The first thing I always notice is the smell. Not the chalk dust or the faint, greasy scent of the cafeteria's pizza, but the smell of the old oak tree outside the library window. It’s a deep, earthy smell, like rain and old books and something green and alive. It calls to me, a low hum in my bones that I have to ignore.

My name is Ethan, and to everyone at Westbrook High, I'm just a quiet kid who likes art class and gets a little too excited about the biology unit on trees. They don't know that my ears are slightly pointed, hidden under a mop of dark hair I never let the barber trim too short. They don't know that my eyes, which they think are just an unusual shade of green, can see the faint, shimmering life-force that pulses through every plant in the city.

It's a secret I've kept my whole life. My parents, who are also wood elves, run a small, unassuming plant shop downtown. We pass as a normal family, just a bit too fond of gardening. But high school is a different kind of forest, one with its own predators and its own strange magic.

Today, the forest is a concrete jungle of lockers and loud voices. I'm trying to get to my biology class, clutching my sketchbook. Inside are drawings of leaves, not the boring diagrams from the textbook, but the intricate veins of a ginkgo leaf, the spiral of a fern frond. I draw them to keep myself grounded.

"Hey, Ethan! Wait up!"

It's Maya. She's in my art class. She's loud and bright, like a sunflower, and she's one of the only people who talks to me without making me feel like a bug under a magnifying glass.

"Did you finish the sketch for the mural?" she asks, falling into step beside me.

"Yeah," I mumble, showing her the page. It's a design for the school's new eco-club mural. A sprawling tree, its roots wrapping around the globe.

"Whoa," she breathes, her eyes wide. "It's so... alive. It looks like you could just walk right into it."

I feel a flush of warmth. She sees it. She sees the life in it. "Thanks. It's just... trees are cool."

She laughs. "You're the only person I know who makes 'cool' sound like a sacred word."

The warning bell rings, and we hurry into class. Mr. Henderson, our biology teacher, is droning on about photosynthesis. I try to focus, but the fluorescent lights are buzzing, and I can feel the two potted plants on the windowsill straining towards the weak sunlight. They're thirsty. I can feel their dryness like a scratch in my own throat.

During the lab, we're supposed to be looking at onion cells under a microscope. I'm paired with a guy named Mark, who's the star of the football team. He's not mean, just oblivious. He's poking at the microscope, frustrated.

"This is so boring," he sighs. "Who cares about cells?"

I look at the slide. The cells are a beautiful, orderly grid, tiny brick walls of life. "They're kind of like... a city," I say, surprising myself. "Each one has a job. They all work together to keep the whole thing alive."

He looks at me, a flicker of interest in his eyes. "Huh. Never thought of it that way."

For a moment, I feel a connection. A tiny bridge between my world and his. But then the bell rings, and the moment is gone.

The hardest part is lunch. The cafeteria is a cacophony of noise and processed food smells. I find a table in the corner, away from the crowd, and pull out my lunch. My mom packed me a sandwich and an apple, but I also have a small container of wild berries I picked from the park on the way to school. They taste like sunshine and home.

I'm halfway through my apple when I see it. A group of kids are gathered around the big oak tree outside, laughing. One of them, a kid named Jake, is pulling at a low-hanging branch. He's not trying to break it, just swinging on it, but I can feel the tree's discomfort. A sharp, splintering ache in my own arm.

I can't help it. I get up and walk outside.

"Hey," I say, my voice quieter than I'd like. "Maybe you shouldn't do that."

Jake stops, looking at me with a smirk. "Why? Scared I'll hurt the big, tough tree?"

"It's... it's old," I say, my heart pounding. "The branch might break. And it's not good for the tree."

He rolls his eyes. "Whatever, tree-hugger." But he lets go of the branch. The tree sighs in relief, a wave of gratitude washing over me.

I walk back inside, my face hot. I hate drawing attention to myself. But I couldn't just stand there.

Later, in art class, I'm lost in my own world, painting the mural. I'm using a deep, forest green, mixing it with a touch of gold to make it shimmer. Maya is painting a bright blue sky next to me.

"You know," she says, not looking up from her canvas, "that was cool what you did at lunch. With Jake."

I freeze. "You saw that?"

"Yeah. He's a jerk. The tree is, like, a hundred years old. It's the best tree on campus." She smiles. "You're a good guy, Ethan. A little weird, but a good guy."

Her words are like a warm breeze. "Thanks," I manage to say.

As the final bell rings, I pack up my things. The hallways are a river of students, all rushing to leave. I'm about to head out when I see a small, wilted plant on the windowsill by the main office. It's a peace lily, its leaves drooping, its soil bone dry. It's been neglected for weeks.

I look around. No one is watching. I walk over, and as casually as I can, I place my hand on the pot. I close my eyes for a second and let a tiny trickle of my own energy flow into the soil. I feel the roots perk up, drinking in the moisture. The leaves slowly, almost imperceptibly, begin to straighten.

I pull my hand away and walk out into the afternoon sun. The air smells like cut grass and car exhaust, but underneath it all, I can still smell the earth. The city is a strange, loud forest, but I'm learning to find my place in it. I'm learning that being a wood elf in high school isn't about hiding who I am. It's about finding the quiet magic in the everyday, and sharing it, one small, secret act at a time. [end of text]


